Model card: diabetes screening points card
Intended use
What it does: ranks adults (20+) by their chance of undiagnosed diabetes, so that the people most at risk are sent for an HbA1c blood test.
Users: community health workers, pharmacies, workplace or camp screening, either on the printed card or with predict.py, which scores one person or a whole CSV.
Not for: diagnosing diabetes, deciding treatment, people already diagnosed, pregnant women, or anyone under 20.
Inputs
Age, height and weight (as BMI), family history of diabetes (parent or sibling), ever told high blood pressure, and regular exercise or hard physical work. No lab values.

Output
An integer score from 0 to 14. A score of 7 or more means "get an HbA1c test".

Training data
NHANES 2017–March 2020: 6,217 non-pregnant US adults who said they had not been diagnosed with diabetes and had HbA1c, height, weight and waist measured. 227 of them had undiagnosed diabetes (HbA1c ≥ 6.5%).

Performance (out-of-fold, 2017–2020)
Metric	Value
AUROC	0.741 (95% CI 0.709–0.771); ADA risk test 0.745 on the same people
Sensitivity at score ≥ 7	89.7%
Share of people sent for a test	52.3%
Undiagnosed diabetes, score ≥ 7	5.9%
Undiagnosed diabetes, score < 7	0.7%
Subgroups (full ML model and ADA test; see notebook section 5c)
Men: with a single threshold the full model catches 72% of cases in men, against 83% for the ADA test. A separate threshold per sex brings both sexes to 84% at no extra overall testing (notebook section 5c). The paper card has a single cut-off; a per-sex cut-off for the card is still to be tested.
NH Asian adults (n = 776, 36 cases): the card's AUROC is 0.765. Asians develop diabetes at lower BMI (mean 28.8 vs 34.9 among cases), so BMI-based points may under-score South Asians. With so few cases, the estimate is uncertain.
Performance by income band was similar (AUROC 0.73–0.77).
Known limitations
The label is a single HbA1c test, not a confirmed diagnosis. HbA1c can mislead in anaemia and haemoglobin variants, which are common in India.
The card is built on US data and has not been validated in India. Cut-offs and risk rates must be re-checked on Indian data (for example NFHS-5) before any programme use.
The risk percentages shown by predict.py are US sample rates. A model trained on 2017–20 over-predicted risk in 2021–23 (3.4% predicted vs 2.5% observed), so re-calibrate before using the card in a new population.
The cut-off was chosen on the same data it is reported on, which makes the reported numbers slightly optimistic.
Ethical considerations
A "lower risk" result must not discourage anyone with symptoms from getting tested; predict.py tells anyone with symptoms to test, whatever the score. The tool should send people to an affordable HbA1c test. Without that next step, screening adds anxiety and no benefit.
